{
  "image_name": "bddf4688572700d01a4e4bbcc1869133.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares three battery configurations that all use a LiCoO2 cathode but differ on the negative-electrode side. In each schematic, the cathode is on an aluminum current collector, the separator sits in the middle, and the opposite side is connected to copper. A lit bulb suggests the cells are delivering current.\n\nThe left panel shows a conventional lithium-ion battery, where lithium ions are stored in a graphite anode. The graphite layers are drawn as stacked sheets, emphasizing intercalation-type storage. The middle panel shows a lithium-metal battery, where the graphite host is replaced by a lithium metal anode next to the separator, indicating direct lithium plating/stripping rather than storage inside graphite. The right panel shows an anode-free lithium battery: there is no pre-existing anode material at assembly, only the copper current collector beside the separator.\n\nTaken together, the image highlights a progression from host-based lithium storage to lithium metal and then to an anode-free design. The main visible comparison is how removing graphite and eventually removing excess lithium on the anode side could simplify the cell and potentially increase energy density by reducing inactive or heavy components.",
